  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gnomes have changed a lot throughout the history of Dungeons and Dragons. At\n\ttimes they’ve been monsters. At times they’ve been fey. In general, DnD has\n\talways had trouble pinning down gnomes thematically in a way that\n\tdistinguishes them from halflings, but I think 5th edition may have done the\n\tbest job of any edition to date. Gnomes have subraces which are very distinct\n\tfrom one another, and their traits offer some unique an interesting\n\toptions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Gnome’s biggest challenge is their ability score increases. All gnomes\n\tshare an Intelligence increase which predisposes them to being artificers and\n\twizards, but generally you’ll only have one or two viable subraces for any\n\tother class. Under the default rules, Gnomes are generally a niche option that\n\tworks well in specific builds, but the Gnome simply doesn’t have the right\n\tability score increases to fill a broad range of character options."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "As with many similar races, the Customizing Your Origin rules introduced in\n\tTasha’s Cauldron of Everything did a lot to open up the Gnome’s build options.\n\tGnome Cunning is a powerful defense, especially for martial characters who are\n\toften vulnerable to mind-affecting hazards like spells, so suddenly the gnome\n\twent from a passable short wizard to an interesting option for mentally\n\tresilient martial builds in addition to being a good spellcaster.\n\tUnfortunately the Rock Gnome and Mark of Scribing become extremely niche\n\toptions, but the Svirfneblin (deep gnome) is good for stealthy builds and the\n\tForest Gnome makes a good base for almost anything else that can put Minor\n\tIllusion to good use."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Perhaps the Gnome’s biggest problem under the custom origin rules is that\n\tthere’s very little diversity. You’re either a sneaky gnome (svirfneblin) or\n\tyour only distinguishing active trait is that you can cast Minor Illuson\n\t(forest gnome). That can feel like an unsatisfying reward for picking gnome as\n\tyour race. Of course, I absolutely love Minor Illusion, so I’ll happily take\n\tit on any character that can get it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Deep Gnome/Svirfneblin referenced in this guide refers to the original version of the subrace. For the updated version of the Deep Gnome published in Monsters of the Multiverse, see our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Deep Gnome Handbook",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/races/deep-gnome/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
