    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Gnome Classes (Customizable Origins)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Minor Illusion isn’t on the Artificer’s spell list, so the Forest Gnome feels\n\tlike an obvious choice here. If you want to be stealthy, an armorer in\n\tinfiltrator armor will make the Svirfneblin’s camouflage obsolete, so the\n\tForest Gnome is really your best bet. Gnome Cunning adds another layer to the\n\tArtificer’s already incredible durability, so the whole combination procudes\n\tan extremely durable, versatile spellcaster. Rock Gnome obviously fits the\n\ttheme better, but from an optimization perspective it’s hard to compete."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Barbarian’s biggest vulnerability is mental saving throws, and Gnome\n\tCunning does a lot to address that critical weakness. Any variety of gnome\n\twill work fine here. Forest Gnome and Mark of Scribing might seem like odd\n\tchoices here since you can’t cast spells while raging, but neither subrace’s\n\tspells are typically useful in combat, instead offering interesting tools to\n\tuse outside of combat, where the Barbarian frequently struggles to make\n\tthemselves useful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-barbarian-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The biggest limitation is that small races can’t use weapons with the Heavy\n\ttrait effectively, so the Greataxe isn’t an option. You’ll likely need to\n\tsettle for using a Versatile weapon like a longsword two-handed, or you can go\n\tPath of the Beast, which doesn’t care about your size."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Gnome adds very little to the Bard beyond Gnome Cunning. Minor Illusion\n\tis already available to bards, so Forest Gnome’s big selling point doesn’t\n\tmatter much. The Svirfneblin’s Superior Darkvision is nice, but Stone\n\tCamouflage is less interesting once your can cast Enhance Ability and get\n\tAdvantage on "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "all"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " Dexterity checks without worrying about terrain."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Minor Illusion isn’t available to clerics, so the Forest Gnome offers and\n\texciting new tool. Gnome Cunning is also a great way to keep your party’s\n\tprimary healer from being mind-controller or banished or whatever else."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Forest Gnome’s Speak Small Beasts feels like a natural choice for the\n\tDruid, but remember that it’s not a subsitute for Speak with Animals. But\n\tdon’t let that deter you: Minor Illusion is amazing and it’s not on the\n\tDruid’s spell list, and Speak With Small Beasts is still a fun thematic\n\telement for a druid. Gnome Cunnin likely applies while using Wild Shape, which\n\tis a great defense for Circle of the Moon."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Fighter’s biggest weakness is mental saving throws, so Gnome Cunning is a\n\thuge benefit for the Fighter. Unless you plan to use weapons with the Heavy\n\tproperty, being small has no impact on your capabilities, so the full range of\n\tfighter subclasses is available to you. You can even take Rune Knight and go\n\tfrom Small to Large (or larger!)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gnome Cunning provides an additional layer of defense for a class that sorely\n\tneeds better defenses at low levels, but saving throws aren’t the Monk’s\n\tbiggest defensive issue. Picking a subrace can also be difficult. Forest Gnome\n\tis good becuase Minor Illusion is good, and Svirfneblin is good if you plan to\n\tbe your party’s Scout."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gnome Cunning on top of Aura of Protection makes you fantastically resilient\n\tto mental effects. Basically any subrace will work. A Dexterity-based\n\tsvirfneblin build could make a decent scout."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Svirfneblin is a great choice for stealty classes like the Ranger,\n\tespecially in subterranean campaigns where rocky terrain is abundant. The\n\tForest Gnome also makes a fine ranger, offering Minor Illusion as a useful\n\ttool to expand the Ranger’s capabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Svirfneblin is a great choice for stealty classes like the Rogue, especially in subterranean campaigns where rocky terrain is abundant. The Forest Gnome also makes a good rogue, offering early access to spells which might normally only be available to the Arcane Trickster."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Sorcerers already get more cantrips than any other base classes, and while\n\tthe Forest Gnome’s and Mark of Scribing’s extra cantrips don’t hurt, they also\n\tdon’t add anything that you couldn’t already get. Svirfnebling is a good\n\tchoice, allowing you to fight in the dark with better range than most\n\tcreatures and allowing you to match the stealth capabilities of your party’s\n\tScout, provided that you’re in a rocky environment. Of course, you can learn\n\tEnhance Ability and make Stone Camouflage irrelevant."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Warlocks get just as many cantrips as most full spellcasters, but they’re\n\tgenerally locked into Eldritch Blast, which limits your options for options\n\twhich can’t be solved by shooting them, so the Forest Gnome and Mark of\n\tScribing offering additional cantrips is appealing."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gnome Cunning is a great defense, especially paired with the Wizard’s\n\tproficiency in both Intelligence and Wisdom saving throws. Unfortunately,\n\tthat’s the biggest benefit. Wizards can already learn Minor Illusion, and\n\tEnhance Ability makes the Svirfneblin’s Stone Camouflage obsolete. Getting an\n\textra cantrip from the Forest Gnome or Mark of Scribing is nice, but it\n\tdoesn’t solve any problems for the Wizard."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Gnome Classes (Default Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Gnomes get the crucial Intelligence increase as a core gnome racial trait, so any subrace works fine. Rock Gnome fits thematically because of the Tinker trait, but you don’t need it to succeed, and the benefits of Tinker are extremely minor. Forest Gnome’s access to Minor Illusion is extremely useful, even if the ability score increases aren’t quite as helpful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Strength increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mark of Scribing offers the crucial Charisma increase, plus some additional spellcasting. The new spell list options aren’t great and you still need to spend spells known to learn them, but you get a free cantrip and some innate spellcasting."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Wisdom increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Wisdom increase. Forest gnomes seem like they would make a natural Druid,\n\tbut the ability scores don’t work and Speak With Small Beasts is more amusing\n\tthan useful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Fighter is your best bet for a front-line melee gnome. Deep Gnome and Forest Gnome both get Dexterity increases, and between a Dexterity increase and Intelligence increase the Eldritch Knight is an obvious choice. Gnome Cunning provides a powerful defense against spells which target your mental saves, which are frequently a problem for front-line martial characters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Forest gnomes get the crucial Dexterity increase that you need to make the\n\tMonk work, but you don’t get much else."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can argue that the Forest Gnome’s Dexterity increase is enough to make a paladin work, but on its own that’s not going to be especially effective. Paladins are very MAD, so you really want increases to two important ability scores."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Deep Gnome and the Forest Gnome both provide Dexterity increases, which\n\tis crucial for the Ranger. Unfortunately, the Ranger benefits very little from\n\tthe Gnome’s other traits."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Deep Gnome and the Forest Gnome both make great rogues of any kind, but\n\tArcane Trickster is a natural fit thanks to the Intelligence increase and the\n\tForest Gnome gives you Minor Illusion so you can get a taste of magic before\n\tyou hit level 3."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mark of Scribing offers a Charisma increase, plus some additional\n\tspellcasting options. Wizard is typically a better option due the Gnome’s base\n\tIntelligence increase, but a +1 Charisma increase is technically the only\n\tthing that the Sorcerer absolutely needs."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mark of Scribing offers a Charisma increase, plus some additional\n\tspellcasting options. Wizard is typically a better option due the Gnome’s base\n\tIntelligence increase, but a +1 Charisma increase is technically the only\n\tthing that the Warlock absolutely needs."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-default-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "default-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-default-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An intelligence increase as a core racial trait means that any gnome subrace\n\tcan make an excellent wizard."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Gnome Feats",
      "sourceAnchor": "feats",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fade-awayxgte",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Fade AwayXGtE",
      "sourceAnchor": "fade-awayxgte",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fade-awayxgte-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A great defensive option, especially for frail classes like the Wizard. If\n\tyou use this early in an enemy’s turn, they might be forced to make attacks\n\tagainst you while you’re invisible, potentially causing them to miss. You can\n\tthen spend your turn to get somewhere safe."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-squat-nimblenessxgte",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Squat NimblenessXGtE",
      "sourceAnchor": "squat-nimblenessxgte",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-squat-nimblenessxgte-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Only useful if you’re extremely worried about being grappled."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-svirfneblin-magiceepc-scag",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Svirfneblin MagicEEPC / SCAG",
      "sourceAnchor": "svirfneblin-magiceepc-scag",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-svirfneblin-magiceepc-scag-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An interesting choice for the Svirfneblin. Already the go-to sneaky gnome, nondetection protects you from divination spells like See Invisibility, so if you can also cast Invisibility you’re very hard to detect without something like truesight or blindsight. Since one of the other spells provided by the feat requires a save from the target, I recommend only taking this on Intelligence-based builds like the Artificer, the Wizard, and possibly the Arcane Trickster Rogue. Those classes will also benefit from the defense provided by Blur, and having Disguise Self available from the feat removes any potential desire to learn and/or prepare it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-dragonmarks",
      "heading": "Gnome Dragonmarks",
      "sourceAnchor": "dragonmarks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dragonmarks-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dragonmarks are detailed in Eberron: Rising from the Last War. Gnomes treat\n\tdragonmarks like a subrace, adding its traits to the core gnome traits."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-mark-of-scribing",
      "parentSectionId": "section-dragonmarks",
      "heading": "Mark of Scribing",
      "sourceAnchor": "mark-of-scribing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mark-of-scribing-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Charisma increase and an Intelligence increase are unusual to find on the\n\tsame race (Tieflings are notably the only other race with the same increases),\n\tand granting a Charisma increase doesn’t do anything to broaden the Gnome’s\n\tappeal beyond classes that depend on casting spells. Sorcerer and Warlock\n\tbecome viable options for the Gnome, the Paladin can work with Fighting Style\n\t(Blessed Warrior)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mark-of-scribing-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Even with the benefits of the custom origin rules, Mark of Scribing is a hard\n\tchoice for an adventurer. For a person in Eberron with a day job it’s\n\texcellent, but for adventurers fighting dragons and crawling dungeons there’s\n\tvery little here thats useful enough to make Mark of Scribing your subrace.\n\tYou technically get more spellcasting than the Forest Gnome, but Message is\n\tsuch a limited and situational spell compared to Minor Illusions that the\n\tForest Gnome is still more broadly appealing."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
